1. dispatch-marker line: 
```ts
test("a dispatch marker in the child's report is not the machinery asking for an agent", () => {
  const body = subIssueReportBody({
    child: 12,
    report: "The work is delivered.\n\n<!-- atomaton:dispatch=engineer -->",
    pullRequests: [],
  });
  expect(parseCommentCommand(proseOnly(body)).agent).toBe("");
  expect(DISPATCH_TAG.hasProse(body)).toBe(false);
  expect(body).toContain("The work is delivered.");
});
```
Hmm — after `withoutTags`, the dispatch line is stripped entirely, so `DISPATCH_TAG.hasProse(body)` false and no ask. That's the first defence. But I want to also pin the second defence (defuseCommandStart) for a marker that survives stripping — e.g. a malformed spelling that `DISPATCH_RE` accepts but `withoutTags` does not strip. DISPATCH_RE accepts `<!--atomaton:dispatch = engineer-->`? Let's see: `^<!--\s*atomaton:dispatch\s*=\s*(AGENT_NAME_PATTERN)\s*-->`. The tag writer pattern in tags.ts is `<!--\\s*atomaton:dispatch=(?:pattern)\\s*-->` (from makeTag, no `\s*` before `=`). So `<!-- atomaton:dispatch =engineer -->` would match DISPATCH_RE (a command to the thread reader) but NOT the registered tag pattern (no whitespace tolerance around `=`), so `withoutTags` leaves it → then `defuseCommandStart` escapes `<` → no longer a command. 

That's a great test of the second defence: a hand-retyped marker. The comment in comment-command.ts even says "The whitespace tolerance around `=` is likewise wider than the tag writer ever emits -- it costs nothing and this is the one place reading a marker a human may have retyped by hand."

So test:
```ts
test("a dispatch marker the tag stripper does not recognise is still not a request", () => {
